{"type":"message","id":"c57bcdf6","parentId":"d5c0d859","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:04:49.369Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_2fsFlD6b7gg6v9fG5YT8VIu3","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"bitcoin-iran-selloff-2026-05-18\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin slides below $77K after Trump Iran warning triggers market sell-off\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin falls to $76K after Trump says \\u2018clock is ticking\\u2019 for Iran\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"GoogleNews\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-falls-to-76k-after-trump-says-clock-is-ticking-for-iran?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets\",\n    \"https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiugFBVV95cUxPdWljMVdkWmhveThjVVVqQkU2RngxQWhGUGVsaVNELUdzWGU2YU1OVmFpMktQd3h2RUdSOHg1dkRZeGdVQ0hyc0JHTWE5OVFSbGRreWRxQlAtZ3pZZTU3LTRIVzZVOVhEM1NhMVRxbVlscm9mV1FfYzUwMVBsRlR0ZDRhb1dOX0tQeDdCSEFFWmExLTJWMnI3ZThFZ2ROaGdUX2ZVYTc0VEpFMWVVLThTNjhmLTNlWi1uNGc?oc=5\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Bitcoin dropped to about $76,500, erasing gains made since May 1 and marking a move back to three-week lows.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph reported more than $607 million in long positions liquidated in the last 24 hours, with Bitcoin longs accounting for about $190 million and total crypto liquidations reaching roughly $677 million.\",\n    \"The sell-off followed fresh US-Iran tensions after Donald Trump warned on Truth Social that the \\u2018clock is ticking\\u2019 for Iran.\",\n    \"CoinDesk\\u2019s RSS headline framed the move as Bitcoin sliding below $77,000 as Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattled risk assets and triggered broad crypto liquidations.\",\n    \"The article said traders were watching $76,000 as a key support level, while some analysis warned BTC could revisit the $65,000 demand area if bears stay in control.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"CoinTelegraph reported that Bitcoin dropped to about $76,500 in early Asian trading on Monday, falling as much as 7% over the previous three days and hitting three-week lows. The move erased the gains BTC had made since May 1 and came after the market had recently pushed Bitcoin to around 13-week highs near $83,000. The article said the shift in sentiment was closely tied to renewed US-Iran tensions. On Sunday, US President Donald Trump posted a warning that the \\u2018clock is ticking\\u2019 for Iran, and the article linked that geopolitical message to a rapid deterioration in crypto risk appetite. CoinTelegraph\\u2019s framing was blunt: Bitcoin\\u2019s bearish momentum had become increasingly tied to geopolitical developments rather than only crypto-native catalysts. The article highlighted that traders were watching the $76,000 area as an important support zone. If that level failed, one analyst suggested BTC could fall toward the $65,000 demand area. The same report said Bitcoin\\u2019s weakness came alongside heavy derivatives damage: more than $607 million in long positions were liquidated over the prior 24 hours, with BTC long liquidations making up about $190 million of that total. CoinTelegraph also said total crypto liquidations reached around $677 million. The article noted that oil markets reacted too, with WTI surging above $103 and briefly touching about $104 per barrel before easing back toward $101. The explanation given was that higher oil prices intensify inflation concerns, reinforce expectations of higher-for-longer interest rates, lift the dollar and Treasury yields, and create a difficult backdrop for speculative assets like Bitcoin. The piece emphasized that this macro and geopolitical squeeze contributed to Bitcoin bears taking back control after a period of optimism driven by spot ETF inflows and enthusiasm around the US CLARITY Act. CoinDesk\\u2019s RSS headline covered the same broad event, describing Bitcoin sliding below $77,000 as Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattled risk assets. Its snippet said the move sent oil higher and triggered broad crypto liquidations. Even though the full CoinDesk article was not directly accessible in this session, the headline and feed summary corroborate the core sequence: Trump\\u2019s Iran warning, a risk-off reaction across markets, and a deep drawdown in Bitcoin and other crypto assets. Google News also surfaced related coverage from multiple outlets, including headlines that described Bitcoin dropping to a two-week low after Trump said the \\u2018clock is ticking\\u2019 for Iran, and another item noting that BTC hits a two-week low as crypto liquidations topped half a billion dollars. A separate Google News item summarized the same market move as a crypto market crash, with roughly $700 million in Bitcoin, Ethereum, XRP, Solana and AI coin liquidations. Together, the sources point to a single dominant story: a fast, geopolitically driven crypto sell-off led by Bitcoin, with liquidation pressure, oil volatility, and rising yields reinforcing the downturn. Traders were focused on whether Bitcoin could hold above the mid-$76,000 range, or whether the market would extend lower toward the next major demand zone.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779109489369}}
