{"type":"message","id":"261c0759","parentId":"ba7f8bcd","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:53:46.721Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It means the **WordPress publisher thought the image download/upload result was only 43 bytes**, which is far too small to be a real JPEG.\n\nIn plain terms:\n\n- the **creator agent succeeded**\n- the real file on disk was fine:\n  - `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg`\n  - about **406 KB**\n  - valid **JPEG**\n- but during the **wp-publisher step**, something in its image-handling flow ended up reading or receiving a tiny **43-byte payload** instead of the real image\n\nThat usually means one of these:\n\n1. **It read the wrong thing**\n   - e.g. a bad temp file, broken intermediate file, or wrong path handling\n\n2. **It got an error response instead of image bytes**\n   - sometimes tools save a short text error/HTML/API response, and the publisher only sees “file exists but tiny”\n\n3. **Symlink/path handling bug**\n   - `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg` is a symlink to the real run file\n   - creator/validator followed it correctly\n   - wp-publisher may be handling that path differently\n\nWhy I say it’s a **wp-publisher bug**:\n- I verified the final file myself after the failure\n- it was still a valid JPEG and full size\n- so the failure is not “image generation failed”\n- the failure is “publisher did not correctly consume that image”\n\nSo the short version is:\n\n**The image was good, but the WordPress publish script incorrectly treated it as a tiny 43-byte file during its upload step.**\n\nIf you want, I can also give you a **technical debug checklist** for fixing the wp-publisher script."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":54298,"output":349,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":54647,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1779112415318,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgshajsngBXzC51vyMlg6bvi4je9b"}}
